@startuml invite-flow
title Invite a user — end to end

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName "Helvetica"

actor Administrator as Admin
participant "api" as Api
database "postgres" as Pg
participant "worker" as Worker
queue "kafka" as K
participant "SMTP" as Smtp
actor Invitee

Admin -> Api : POST /api/v1/users/invitations
activate Api
Api -> Api : InviteUserCommand\nvalidate · check address is free
Api -> Api : UserInvitationService.Invite\nUser(Invited) + Invitation(token hash only)
Api -> Pg : one transaction:\nusers + invitations + outbox_messages
Api --> Admin : 201 Created
deactivate Api

Worker -> Pg : poll outbox
activate Worker
Worker -> K : <prefix>.user.invited
deactivate Worker

K -> Worker : UserInvitedConsumer
activate Worker
Worker -> K : <prefix>.email.requested
note right of Worker
  Deciding to send and actually sending
  are separate hops, so a broken SMTP
  server cannot stall user events.
end note
deactivate Worker

K -> Worker : EmailDispatchConsumer
activate Worker
Worker -> Worker : claim idempotency key (Redis SET NX)\nrender template
Worker -> Smtp : send
deactivate Worker
Smtp -> Invitee : invitation email\nwith accept link

Invitee -> Api : POST /api/v1/auth/invitations/accept\n(token, password)
activate Api
Api -> Pg : consume invitation · activate user\nissue refresh token
Api --> Invitee : 200 + access & refresh tokens
deactivate Api

note over Invitee
  Accepting signs them straight in.
  They just proved possession of a
  single-use secret.
end note

@enduml
